-- D6 state legislature, the deferred slice: 25 states' Senates + North
-- Dakota's House + Nebraska's unicameral Legislature -- every chamber that
-- was explicitly excluded from db/ingest/openstates-legislature.mjs
-- (migrations 062/b715fe3-era) because it genuinely staggers: only part
-- of the chamber is up each election cycle, assigned by a district-number
-- cohort that OpenStates' own /people endpoint cannot disambiguate (it
-- has no term-start field at all -- see that ingester's own header
-- comment for the full reasoning behind deferring these at the time).
--
-- Different technique than the ingester, not the same one applied harder:
-- rather than trying to derive a district-parity rule per state (shown
-- at the time to be genuinely unstable across a redistricting decade,
-- e.g. California's own parity flipped after 2021), this migration reads
-- each individual current member's own real "Date assumed office" from
-- Ballotpedia's per-chamber roster page (the same Office/Name/Party/Date
-- table format already used throughout this project for executive
-- offices) -- sidestepping the whole inference problem by using a real,
-- individually-sourced date per person instead of a computed one. Spot
-- check: California's own roster confirms the staggering is real and
-- exactly as expected (District 1: Dec 2024; District 2: Dec 2014,
-- alternating pattern across districts) -- not just trusted blind.
--
-- North Dakota pairs each of its 47 Senate districts with 2 House seats
-- from the same district (94 House seats total) -- both chambers were
-- fully deferred, not just the Senate, since North Dakota staggers both.
-- Nebraska's single chamber is officially named "Legislature" but its own
-- members carry the real title "Senator" (confirmed live via OpenStates
-- earlier this session too), same "State Senator" title used here.
--
-- Term length is 4 years for every row, matching NCSL's own published
-- data for all of these chambers (the earlier ingester's STATE_TERM_INFO
-- comment already established this) -- six of these states (Arkansas,
-- Delaware, Florida, Hawaii, Illinois, Texas) have a "2-4-4" irregular
-- individual-term-length pattern tied to each decade's redistricting
-- cycle (a member's own term might genuinely be 2 years right after
-- redistricting, then 4 thereafter), but the OFFICE's nominal/
-- constitutional term length is 4 years, same representation this
-- project has already used for other mid-transition offices (e.g.
-- Alabama's Public Service Commission in migration 066).
--
-- Real vacancies preserved, not filled -- same discipline as every prior
-- migration.



